### Why do retries multiply instead of add?

A retry at a given layer re-executes everything below it. Layer A's three attempts each trigger a full B sequence, and each B attempt triggers a full C sequence. The total at the bottom is the product, not the sum.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

public class RetryMultiplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int layerA = 3, layerB = 3, layerC = 3;

        int multiplied = layerA * layerB * layerC;
        int added = layerA + layerB + layerC;

        System.out.println("Retries multiply: " + layerA + " x " + layerB + " x " + layerC
                + " = " + multiplied + " attempts");
        System.out.println("Common misconception (add): " + layerA + " + " + layerB + " + " + layerC
                + " = " + added + " attempts");

        // Add a 4th layer
        int layerD = 3;
        System.out.println("Add 4th layer: " + multiplied * layerD + " attempts (exponential!)");
    }
}
```

```text
Retries multiply: 3 x 3 x 3 = 27 attempts
Common misconception (add): 3 + 3 + 3 = 9 attempts
Add 4th layer: 81 attempts (exponential!)
```

### Why is this invisible under healthy conditions?

Retries are conditional on failure. When everything is healthy, the first attempt succeeds, so no layer reaches its second attempt. The 27x factor is latent: it exists in the code but never executes.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

public class LatentAmplifier {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("=== Healthy Conditions ===");
        System.out.println("1 user request → 1 downstream call (first attempt succeeds)");
        System.out.println("Monitoring: 1:1 ratio. Looks perfect.");
        System.out.println("The 27x factor is latent, invisible.");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("=== Degradation ===");
        System.out.println("1 user request → up to 27 downstream calls (all retries fire)");
        System.out.println("100 user requests → up to 2700 downstream calls");
        System.out.println("Retries become the DOMINANT traffic source.");
    }
}
```

```text
=== Healthy Conditions ===
1 user request → 1 downstream call (first attempt succeeds)
Monitoring: 1:1 ratio. Looks perfect.
The 27x factor is latent, invisible.

=== Degradation ===
1 user request → up to 27 downstream calls (all retries fire)
100 user requests → up to 2700 downstream calls
Retries become the DOMINANT traffic source.
```

### How does this cause a cascading failure?

The dependency is slow, so retries fire, so the dependency gets more load, so it gets slower, so more retries fire. A positive feedback loop that finishes the system off.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

public class RetryFeedbackLoop {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int baseLoad = 100;
        double failureRate = 0.5;
        int retryFactor = 27;

        int attemptedRetries = (int) (baseLoad * failureRate * retryFactor);
        int totalLoad = baseLoad + attemptedRetries;

        System.out.println("Base load: " + baseLoad + " requests");
        System.out.println("Failure rate: " + (failureRate * 100) + "%");
        System.out.println("Retry amplification: " + retryFactor + "x");
        System.out.println("Total load on dependency: " + totalLoad);
        System.out.println("→ dependency now has " + totalLoad + "x the capacity to handle");
        System.out.println("→ failure rate increases → more retries → more load → collapse");
    }
}
```

```text
Base load: 100 requests
Failure rate: 50.0%
Retry amplification: 27x
Total load on dependency: 1450
→ dependency now has 1450x the capacity to handle
→ failure rate increases → more retries → more load → collapse
```

### How do you fix it: retry at one layer only?

Retry at the edge (closest to the user) and make all inner layers fail fast without retrying. This collapses the product to a single factor.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

public class SingleLayerRetry {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("=== WRONG: every layer retries ===");
        System.out.println("Gateway(3) x Transfer(3) x Ledger(3) = 27 attempts");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("=== CORRECT: only edge retries ===");
        System.out.println("Gateway(3) x Transfer(1) x Ledger(1) = 3 attempts");
        System.out.println("Inner layers fail fast, no retry → product collapses");
    }
}
```

```text
=== WRONG: every layer retries ===
Gateway(3) x Transfer(3) x Ledger(3) = 27 attempts

=== CORRECT: only edge retries ===
Gateway(3) x Transfer(1) x Ledger(1) = 3 attempts
Inner layers fail fast, no retry → product collapses
```

### What is a global retry budget?

Cap retries as a fraction of total traffic. Even when everything is failing, amplification is bounded.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

public class RetryBudget {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int totalRequests = 1000;
        double budgetPercent = 10.0;
        int maxRetries = (int) (totalRequests * budgetPercent / 100);

        System.out.println("Total requests: " + totalRequests);
        System.out.println("Retry budget: " + budgetPercent + "%");
        System.out.println("Max allowed retries: " + maxRetries);
        System.out.println("Even under 100% failure, retries capped at " + maxRetries);
        System.out.println("Compare without budget: up to " + totalRequests * 27 + " attempts");
    }
}
```

```text
Total requests: 1000
Retry budget: 10.0%
Max allowed retries: 100
Even under 100% failure, retries capped at 100
Compare without budget: up to 27000 attempts
```

### Why must you propagate deadlines?

Once the overall request deadline has passed, no layer should retry because the result will not be used. Propagating the deadline prevents wasting work on expired requests.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

import java.time.Instant;

public class DeadlinePropagation {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Instant deadline = Instant.now().plusMillis(500);

        System.out.println("Request deadline: 500ms from now");
        System.out.println("After 600ms (deadline passed):");
        System.out.println("  Without propagation: inner layers still retry (waste!)");
        System.out.println("  With propagation: all layers stop → no wasted work");
    }
}
```

```text
Request deadline: 500ms from now
After 600ms (deadline passed):
  Without propagation: inner layers still retry (waste!)
  With propagation: all layers stop → no wasted work
```

### Why must you load-test the degraded path?

A happy-path load test never reveals the 27x amplifier because retries never fire. You must inject failures at the deepest dependency to expose it.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

public class DegradedPathTest {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Happy-path load test: 10,000 req → 10,000 downstream calls. Looks great.");
        System.out.println("Degraded-path test (inject ledger failure):");
        System.out.println("  10,000 req → up to 270,000 downstream calls (27x amplification!)");
        System.out.println("  This ONLY appears when you test with failures injected.");
        System.out.println("  If you never test the degraded path, the amplifier stays hidden.");
    }
}
```

```text
Happy-path load test: 10,000 req → 10,000 downstream calls. Looks great.
Degraded-path test (inject ledger failure):
  10,000 req → up to 270,000 downstream calls (27x amplification!)
  This ONLY appears when you test with failures injected.
  If you never test the degraded path, the amplifier stays hidden.
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How does a circuit breaker complement a retry budget?
- Where in the call chain should the retry live and why?
- How do you make the amplification factor observable in production?
- What is the difference between exponential backoff and a retry budget?
